1
00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:03.933
In this lesson, you'll learn
how to leverage the multi-agent design

2
00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:07.000
to finish a task involving multiple steps.

3
00:00:07.300 --> 00:00:10.900
You'll build a sequence of tasks
between multiple agents

4
00:00:11.100 --> 00:00:16.000
who collaborate to provide a fun customer
onboarding experience for a product.

5
00:00:16.200 --> 00:00:19.533
You also experience
how humans could be seamlessly

6
00:00:19.533 --> 00:00:22.533
involved in the loop of an AI system.

7
00:00:22.533 --> 00:00:25.133
All right. Let's code.

8
00:00:25.366 --> 00:00:27.966
Let's consider a customer
onboarding scenario.

9
00:00:28.266 --> 00:00:31.500
In this scenario,
a typical procedure is to first collect

10
00:00:31.500 --> 00:00:33.166
some customer information
and then survey customer's interest

11
00:00:33.366 --> 00:00:35.500
some customer information
and then survey customer's interest

12
00:00:36.133 --> 00:00:39.700
and then engage with the customer
based on the collected information.

13
00:00:40.233 --> 00:00:44.300
With this in mind, it's a good idea
to decompose this customer

14
00:00:44.300 --> 00:00:46.833
onboarding task into three sub-tasks,

15
00:00:47.266 --> 00:00:49.433
including information collecting,

16
00:00:50.233 --> 00:00:51.433
interest survey,

17
00:00:51.766 --> 00:00:53.566
and customer engagement.

18
00:00:54.533 --> 00:00:57.733
Let's now check
how we could finish this series of tasks

19
00:00:57.866 --> 00:00:59.500
with sequential chat.

20
00:01:01.266 --> 00:01:05.600
So here, we'll use the conversable
agent class from AutoGen

21
00:01:05.766 --> 00:01:07.066
to realize those agents.

22
00:01:09.566 --> 00:01:10.166
Let's first

23
00:01:10.166 --> 00:01:13.166
import this conversable agent,
from AutoGen.

24
00:01:14.166 --> 00:01:17.966
And then the first step
is to create an onboarding agent

25
00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:21.000
that asks for personal information.

26
00:01:21.733 --> 00:01:25.200
And here we realize that
by setting the system message

27
00:01:25.200 --> 00:01:28.566
as a customer onboarding agent,
and gave detailed instruction.

28
00:01:29.566 --> 00:01:34.566
And here we are setting in human
input mode as "never" because we are using

29
00:01:34.766 --> 00:01:38.566
a large language model
to generator response from this agent.

30
00:01:41.333 --> 00:01:44.333
And similarly, let's create
another onboarding agent

31
00:01:44.400 --> 00:01:47.033
that ask for topic preference.

32
00:01:47.366 --> 00:01:50.233
And similarly we can realize,

33
00:01:50.233 --> 00:01:53.566
what do we want to achieve
by setting this system message.

34
00:01:54.233 --> 00:01:57.233
So here, the system message is set to ask

35
00:01:57.333 --> 00:02:00.800
the customer's interest,
and preference on the topics.

36
00:02:01.533 --> 00:02:04.866
And similarly
we will use "never" as human input mode

37
00:02:05.033 --> 00:02:09.666
and use a large language model,
to back this conversable agent.

38
00:02:12.166 --> 00:02:13.166
And another agent

39
00:02:13.166 --> 00:02:17.666
we want to create is the customer
engagement agent that provides fun facts,

40
00:02:17.766 --> 00:02:20.866
jokes, or interesting stories
based on the user's

41
00:02:21.100 --> 00:02:24.100
customer's personal information,
and the topic preference.

42
00:02:24.766 --> 00:02:29.766
And again, we, give detailed instructions,
on the behavior of this engagement

43
00:02:30.066 --> 00:02:33.066
agent by setting the proper
system message.

44
00:02:34.700 --> 00:02:38.600
Now, next,
we should define a customer proxy

45
00:02:38.600 --> 00:02:42.200
agent to act as a proxy
for the real customer.

46
00:02:42.700 --> 00:02:46.000
Note that here we are setting
the human input mode

47
00:02:46.100 --> 00:02:50.300
as "always",
so that this proxy agent could always

48
00:02:50.300 --> 00:02:53.300
solicit human
input from the real customer.

49
00:02:54.800 --> 00:02:58.600
So with those agents constructed,
we can now craft a sequential chat

50
00:02:58.600 --> 00:03:01.600
to finish the onboarding process.

51
00:03:02.500 --> 00:03:03.800
So in this specific

52
00:03:03.800 --> 00:03:08.400
example, each chat is effectively
a two-agent chat between a particular

53
00:03:08.400 --> 00:03:11.066
onboarding agent and custom proxy
agent in each chat.

54
00:03:12.266 --> 00:03:16.666
In each chat, the sender agent sends an initial message

55
00:03:16.666 --> 00:03:20.100
to the recipient to kick off the conversation, and then they will

56
00:03:20.100 --> 00:03:23.300
have a back-and-forth conversation
until the max turns

57
00:03:23.300 --> 00:03:26.300
is reached
or the termination message is received.

58
00:03:27.200 --> 00:03:28.066
In the first chat,

59
00:03:28.066 --> 00:03:30.200
we are setting the max turns to two,

60
00:03:30.200 --> 00:03:33.466
so that they can have
at most two turns of conversation.

61
00:03:34.366 --> 00:03:35.166
In addition,

62
00:03:35.166 --> 00:03:39.666
in the sequential chat scenario,
the tasks, typically depend on each other.

63
00:03:39.900 --> 00:03:42.800
Therefore,
when they want to summarize information

64
00:03:42.800 --> 00:03:45.766
from the previous chat
to be used in the next chat.

65
00:03:46.300 --> 00:03:49.266
In this case,
we want to use the summary method.

66
00:03:49.466 --> 00:03:53.266
In this example, we are using reflection
as the summary method

67
00:03:53.266 --> 00:03:54.966
to summarize the customer's

68
00:03:54.966 --> 00:03:59.266
personal information from the first
chat and pass it to the second chat.

69
00:03:59.766 --> 00:04:02.766
We further add a summary prompt as a way

70
00:04:03.000 --> 00:04:08.000
to instruct the large language model on
how to do the summary.

71
00:04:08.466 --> 00:04:11.366
So basically here
we are instructing it to return

72
00:04:11.366 --> 00:04:15.166
the customer information
into a json object, with this format,

73
00:04:15.466 --> 00:04:18.466
including a field of name
and the location.

74
00:04:20.166 --> 00:04:23.533
So in the second chat,
we are doing similar things. Here,

75
00:04:23.533 --> 00:04:26.633
the sender is the onboarding topic
preference agent.

76
00:04:26.766 --> 00:04:29.766
And the recipient
is a customer proxy agent.

77
00:04:30.666 --> 00:04:32.300
The kickoff message is:

78
00:04:32.300 --> 00:04:36.466
"Great, could you tell me what topics you are
interested in reading about?"

79
00:04:36.866 --> 00:04:40.366
And the summary method
we are using reflection with LLM

80
00:04:40.600 --> 00:04:44.333
and at this time we are not providing
any customized summary prompt.

81
00:04:44.600 --> 00:04:47.833
And that will be using the building
default prompt.

82
00:04:48.000 --> 00:04:52.400
And here in this case the max turn
is set to be one. Because we just want

83
00:04:52.533 --> 00:04:57.300
to use one turn of conversation to seek
the customer's interested topic.

84
00:04:58.133 --> 00:05:00.766
And in your specific case,
you can always change

85
00:05:00.766 --> 00:05:04.333
the multiple turns
so that you can seek more information.

86
00:05:05.600 --> 00:05:09.800
And in the third chat,
we have a chat between the customer

87
00:05:09.833 --> 00:05:12.833
proxy agent
and the customer engagement agent.

88
00:05:13.600 --> 00:05:18.600
And this time, it is the customer proxy
agent, who kicks off the conversation.

89
00:05:19.200 --> 00:05:22.266
So basically by saying,
"Let's find something to read."

90
00:05:22.966 --> 00:05:26.366
And after crafting this chat session,
we are ready to

91
00:05:26.666 --> 00:05:30.500
start the whole onboarding process
that executing initiate chats.

92
00:05:32.333 --> 00:05:35.333
In this
example, you will act as the customer.

93
00:05:35.433 --> 00:05:38.966
And when you are prompted for answers,
you should tap in your response

94
00:05:38.966 --> 00:05:42.033
and press enter.
Okay, let's start the chat.

95
00:05:42.633 --> 00:05:46.166
So here, you can see that
we are starting our first chat,

96
00:05:46.166 --> 00:05:47.366
which is between

97
00:05:47.366 --> 00:05:51.433
the onboarding personal information agent
and the customer proxy agent.

98
00:05:52.400 --> 00:05:55.100
Here the onboarding agent is asking

99
00:05:55.100 --> 00:05:58.100
the customer
"What is the name and the location."

100
00:05:58.100 --> 00:06:00.900
Let's say, I'll use a fake name.

101
00:06:00.900 --> 00:06:02.166
Alice.

102
00:06:03.233 --> 00:06:06.233
Next, it is asking, my location.

103
00:06:06.633 --> 00:06:09.633
I say my location is New York.

104
00:06:10.433 --> 00:06:15.300
And here you can see that we are starting
a new chat, which is the second chat

105
00:06:15.300 --> 00:06:19.933
session between the topic preference agent
and the customer proxy agent.

106
00:06:22.666 --> 00:06:26.366
And here, it is asking me to tell it

107
00:06:26.566 --> 00:06:29.900
the topics
that I'm interested in reading about.

108
00:06:30.466 --> 00:06:34.466
So let's say the topic I want to read
is about dog.

109
00:06:34.766 --> 00:06:38.766
And now that you can see
we are entering our third chat session,

110
00:06:39.066 --> 00:06:42.066
and this chat session is between
the customer

111
00:06:42.066 --> 00:06:45.066
proxy agent and the customer engagement agent.

112
00:06:45.166 --> 00:06:48.500
This is the message from the customer
engagement agent.

113
00:06:48.566 --> 00:06:51.566
So basically, you can see that it is using

114
00:06:51.666 --> 00:06:55.466
my personal information,
my name and the location and also

115
00:06:55.466 --> 00:06:59.966
the topic I'm interested in to
have some engagement in the process.

116
00:07:01.300 --> 00:07:04.466
Feel free to pause here, and play around with it.

117
00:07:04.800 --> 00:07:07.866
For example, you can provide your own preferences.

118
00:07:09.300 --> 00:07:09.600
Okay,

119
00:07:09.600 --> 00:07:13.400
so when the chat is done,
you could always check the results

120
00:07:13.400 --> 00:07:16.600
from each of these chat sessions
in the sequential chat.

121
00:07:16.933 --> 00:07:19.933
So here let's chat in this chat session.

122
00:07:21.733 --> 00:07:25.133
And here you can see that
we first have a nest json

123
00:07:25.133 --> 00:07:27.800
format of the customer's
personal information.

124
00:07:28.566 --> 00:07:31.900
And we have a summary,
the customer's interest,

125
00:07:32.200 --> 00:07:36.566
which is Alice is interested
in reading about dog-related topics.

126
00:07:36.866 --> 00:07:39.700
And we also have a nest

127
00:07:39.700 --> 00:07:42.466
engagement message with the customer.

128
00:07:43.133 --> 00:07:46.133
Similarly, you can also check the cost

129
00:07:46.133 --> 00:07:49.133
associated with each of these chats.

130
00:07:50.100 --> 00:07:53.000
From this result,
you can see their detailed,

131
00:07:53.000 --> 00:07:57.800
cost information, including,
the total cost, the prompt token cost,

132
00:07:57.800 --> 00:08:01.133
and the completion token cost,
and the total cost.

133
00:08:02.800 --> 00:08:03.233
Alright.

134
00:08:03.233 --> 00:08:04.033
In this lesson,

135
00:08:04.033 --> 00:08:08.600
we have learned how to use sequential chats
to finish a sequence of dependent tasks.

136
00:08:09.133 --> 00:08:13.366
In the next lesson, you will learn
how to realize the reflection

137
00:08:13.366 --> 00:08:17.333
agentic design pattern with nested chat.
Where a chat

138
00:08:17.533 --> 00:08:21.700
or a sequence of chats are nested
within another chat

139
00:08:22.000 --> 00:08:23.900
as in the monologue of an agent.

